{
  "deliverable": "out/scales-and-claws-of-shanghai.epub",
  "title_en": "Scales and Claws of Shanghai",
  "title_zh": "上海鱗爪",
  "author_en": "Yu Muxia",
  "author_zh": "郁慕俠",
  "year": 1933,
  "publisher": "Huaguang Book Company, Shanghai",
  "description": "One hundred sixty-seven short sketches of Shanghai street life in the 1920s and 30s by the newspaperman Yu Muxia: the brothels and the tea houses, the swindles and the slang, the beggars, doctors, fortune-tellers, kidnappers and counterfeiters of the treaty-port city, each caught in a page or two. First published in 1933 as the 'customs' half of Shanghai linzhao (Scales and Claws of Shanghai), a bestselling insider's miscellany of the city; translated here complete, with notes for the reader who knows no Chinese.",
  "language": "en",
  "publication_date": "1933-01-01",
  "translator_note": [
    "This is a complete English translation of <i>Shanghai linzhao</i> (《上海鱗爪》, literally “Scales and Claws of Shanghai”), a collection of one hundred sixty-seven short sketches of Shanghai life by the newspaperman Yu Muxia, first published in Shanghai in 1933.",
    "The translation was made from the 2019 Taipei reprint edited by Cai Dengshan (the “customs volume” of a two-volume reset of the original), an image-only scan with no digital text layer. The Chinese text was recovered by optical character recognition and verified against the page images; names, numbers, and every passage the recognition flagged as uncertain were checked by eye against magnified crops of the scan. Damaged or uncertain readings, where they occur, are declared in the notes rather than silently repaired.",
    "The reprint opens with a modern introduction by its editor, which is recent copyrighted work and is not included here; the author’s own 1933 preface, by his friend Chen Diexian (writing as Tianxuwosheng), is translated in full. Footnotes supply what a reader without Chinese or a background in Chinese history would otherwise miss; they are the translator’s throughout."
  ],
  "source_note": "Image-only PDF scan, 252 pages, of the 2019 Taipei reprint (Xinrui Wenchuang, ed. Cai Dengshan), which resets the 1933 Shanghai text in horizontal-folio, vertical-RTL Traditional Chinese. PDF carries 170 bookmarks (cover, modern introduction, preface, and the essays), verified accurate against the scan. Printed folio = PDF page - 2, constant front to back (checked at pdf 13, 23, 120, 247). Front matter: cover 1-2, modern introduction (daodu) by Cai Dengshan 5-11(pdf), author's preface pdf 12-13, TOC pdf 15-22, blank versos pdf 4/14. Body pdf 23-247 (printed 21-245); pdf 248-249 blank, 250 CIP page, 252 back cover. The 2019 introduction is modern copyrighted text and is EXCLUDED from the translation (noted in the translator's note); the reprint-added period photographs with modern captions are likewise excluded by default, pending the commissioner's call.",
  "pdf_end": 247,
  "printed_end": 245,
